#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427562} :activity/id #uuid "81a180d3-6c3b-57f0-9044-6bd27b374a8d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222 :topic/id #uuid "e4770b01-d5aa-5267-93ca-20f83709e3b1" :topic/math-academy-id 1855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5646c621-9ff4-5c1e-8500-97102b3d30e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427545} {:answer/id #uuid "830afe53-3ec3-5e7b-9c3c-099ac06955ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427546} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac2a6dd-782d-5d5d-9fa2-a488442ba8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427547} {:answer/id #uuid "deb5edd7-a250-52b2-967e-5c46f246d2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427553} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e5049e-edad-5162-bbb3-262144109a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427555}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427553} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b0b14b-3418-5c43-b479-6c5ffa179aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd91b565-0528-53ef-9896-e748c3fd0d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75068" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19a71a95-255d-5e73-bf84-db0c055cc314" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b48259-01c4-5109-abc6-4dc67b3a3146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427541} {:answer/id #uuid "b70926c5-b359-5ad0-bd7d-b705946cb78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427548} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4b0fe3-0884-5aa4-93af-8002f7bc5198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427549} {:answer/id #uuid "d713060d-1719-5dcc-a1f8-8b656c01f943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427552} {:answer/id #uuid "f441b186-2f4b-5de4-917f-48c65dfbcc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427549} :answer-field/id #uuid "a651d1bb-f837-5587-b77d-257e4a384de7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0010b93b-f332-5a69-9522-ddec46829e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-110852" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ce228df-2e95-52de-a0e7-a808a0d5cd03" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f11080-8e79-55d1-814d-39c81b12ba97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427542} {:answer/id #uuid "15dddeb9-e2d3-55d4-9c4e-ed6b802b40f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427543} {:answer/id #uuid "399d1ce8-2eb3-525d-b9b3-45ffc5c60921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427544} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bc1d3c-02a8-5212-85ed-899170ed9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427550} {:answer/id #uuid "cca65a09-cc23-5ec7-990f-956f30407ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427550} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01e595b-9131-553f-970b-e23a7aab76e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7ffe635-df11-5df3-b5e3-505ac6562519" :question/math-academy-id "q-110895" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "1f299c67-ea42-572c-80e2-7c614418b73d"}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427540}]}
